### Drum (Shoe) Brakes {#sec:drum-brakes-14}

While disc brakes dominate modern front-axle applications, **drum brakes** remain common on rear axles of economy vehicles, light trucks, and trailers. They are also widely used in industrial machinery — conveyor brakes, hoists, winches, and crane load brakes — where their self-energizing capability and fail-safe properties are valuable.

#### Components of a Drum Brake {#sec:drum-brake-components-14}

| Component | Function |
|:----------|:---------|
| **Brake Drum** | Rotating cylinder fixed to the wheel hub; provides the cylindrical interior friction surface |
| **Brake Shoes** | Curved metal platforms lined with friction material; press outward against the drum interior |
| **Wheel Cylinder** | Hydraulic actuator that pushes the shoe ends outward when brake pressure is applied |
| **Return Springs** | Pull shoes away from drum when pressure is released; maintain running clearance |
| **Hold-Down Springs** | Keep the shoes located against the backing plate |
| **Adjuster** | Maintains proper shoe-to-drum clearance as the friction lining wears |
| **Backing Plate** | Fixed plate bolted to the axle; provides the pivot (anchor) point for each brake shoe |

#### How Drum Brakes Work {#sec:drum-brake-operation-14}

When the brake pedal is pressed, hydraulic pressure in the wheel cylinder pushes both shoe ends outward simultaneously. The friction lining contacts the rotating drum interior, generating a friction force that opposes drum rotation and decelerates the wheel.

When the pedal is released, the return springs pull both shoes back away from the drum, restoring the running clearance. Unlike disc brakes, drum brakes require periodic **manual or automatic adjustment** to compensate for lining wear — if the clearance grows too large, the pedal stroke becomes excessive.

#### Leading and Trailing Shoes {#sec:leading-trailing-14}

The most important concept in drum brake analysis is that the two shoes in a drum brake perform very differently, even though they receive the same hydraulic force. The difference is entirely due to **geometry relative to drum rotation direction**.

**Leading Shoe (Self-Energizing):**

When the pivot geometry is arranged so that drum rotation *drags the shoe into firmer contact with the drum*, the shoe is **leading**. The friction force between shoe and drum creates a moment about the pivot that amplifies the applied clamping force — like a wedge that tightens as it is pulled. This is the **self-energizing effect**. A smaller hydraulic force produces a larger normal force, and therefore more braking torque, than geometry alone would suggest.

**Trailing Shoe (De-Energizing):**

When the pivot geometry is arranged so that drum rotation *tends to push the shoe away from the drum*, the shoe is **trailing**. The friction force now opposes the applied clamping force. More hydraulic pressure is required to achieve the same braking effect.

**Drum brake shoe arrangements:**

| Arrangement | Shoes | Characteristics |
|:-----------|:------|:----------------|
| **Leading-Trailing (LT)** | 1 leading, 1 trailing | Most common; same performance forward and reverse; leading shoe wears faster |
| **Dual-Leading (LL)** | 2 leading | Stronger braking forward; both shoes become trailing in reverse — very weak |
| **Duo-Servo** | Both act together in series | Extremely powerful self-energizing — one shoe energizes the other; used on some parking brakes and older rear axles |

::: {.callout-industry}
**Industry Application:** North American light trucks (e.g., Ford F-150, Ram 1500) commonly use rear drum brakes on base trims because they are significantly less expensive to manufacture and are adequate for the lower braking demand at the rear axle. The leading-trailing arrangement also provides the same braking performance whether the vehicle is moving forward or reversing a trailer — an important consideration for truck applications.
:::

#### The Shoe Brake Lever Model {#sec:shoe-brake-model-14}

To analyse the forces in a shoe brake quantitatively, each shoe is modelled as a **rigid lever** pivoting about the fixed anchor point D. Three forces act on the lever:

- $F$ — the actuating force from the wheel cylinder, applied at the shoe tip
- $F_N$ — the normal (clamping) force between shoe lining and drum
- $F_R = \mu \cdot F_N$ — the friction force at the drum contact surface


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Leading shoe lever diagram. Pivot D is above the drum contact point. For clockwise drum rotation (Rechtslauf), the friction force F_R acts to rotate the shoe lever in the clamping direction about D — the shoe self-energizes. Dimension l is the total lever length; l_1 is the moment arm of F_N; l_2 is the moment arm of F_R about D.](images/drTrain-image22.png){fig-align='center' width=62%}
:::
:::



::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Trailing shoe lever diagram. Pivot D is repositioned so that the friction force F_R acts to rotate the shoe lever away from the drum — the shoe de-energizes. A larger applied force F is required to achieve the same normal force F_N as the leading shoe.](images/drTrain-image23.png){fig-align='center' width=62%}
:::
:::


#### Force Equations — Moment Equilibrium {#sec:shoe-equations-14}

Taking moments about pivot D for each shoe:

**Leading shoe** (friction adds to clamping moment):

\begin{equation}
F \cdot l + F_R \cdot l_2 = F_N \cdot l_1
(\#eq:leading-moment-14)
\end{equation}

Substituting $F_R = \mu \cdot F_N$ and solving for $F$:

\begin{equation}
\boxed{F = F_N \cdot \frac{l_1 - \mu \, l_2}{l}} \qquad \text{(leading shoe)}
(\#eq:leading-shoe-14)
\end{equation}

**Trailing shoe** (friction opposes clamping moment):

\begin{equation}
F \cdot l = F_N \cdot l_1 + F_R \cdot l_2 = F_N \left( l_1 + \mu \, l_2 \right)
(\#eq:trailing-moment-14)
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\boxed{F = F_N \cdot \frac{l_1 + \mu \, l_2}{l}} \qquad \text{(trailing shoe)}
(\#eq:trailing-shoe-14)
\end{equation}

The general form combining both cases using the $\pm$ notation is shown in Figure \@ref(fig:shoe-formula-general-14):


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![General shoe brake force equation. The minus sign applies to the leading shoe (self-energizing); the plus sign applies to the trailing shoe (de-energizing).](images/drTrain-image17.png){fig-align='center' width=38%}
:::
:::


**Symbol definitions:**

| Symbol | Meaning | Units |
|:-------|:--------|:------|
| $F$ | Actuating force at the lever (from wheel cylinder) | N |
| $F_N$ | Normal force between shoe lining and drum | N |
| $F_R$ | Friction force at shoe-drum contact ($= \mu \cdot F_N$) | N |
| $\mu$ | Coefficient of friction (lining on drum) | — |
| $l$ | Total lever length, pivot D to $F$ application point | mm or m |
| $l_1$ | Moment arm of $F_N$ about pivot D | mm or m |
| $l_2$ | Moment arm of $F_R$ about pivot D | mm or m |
| $r$ | Drum radius | mm or m |

**Braking torque** produced by one shoe:

\begin{equation}
\boxed{T = \mu \cdot F_N \cdot r}
(\#eq:shoe-torque-14)
\end{equation}

#### Special Case: Symmetric Pivot ($l_2 = 0$) {#sec:shoe-l2zero-14}

When the pivot D is positioned at the same height as the drum centreline, the friction force $F_R$ passes directly through D and has zero moment arm. The shoe behaves identically regardless of drum rotation direction:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Special case with l_2 = 0. The friction force passes through the pivot and creates no moment. The shoe has no self-energizing or de-energizing tendency — useful for bidirectional brake applications such as industrial hoists where the load can rotate in either direction.](images/drTrain-image24.png){fig-align='center' width=62%}
:::
:::



::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Simplified force equation when l_2 = 0. With no friction moment, the relationship between actuating force and normal force is purely geometric.](images/drTrain-image19.png){fig-align='center' width=25%}
:::
:::


#### Self-Locking Condition {#sec:self-locking-14}

For the **leading shoe**, examine Equation \@ref(eq:leading-shoe-14). As $\mu \cdot l_2$ approaches $l_1$, the denominator approaches zero — $F_N$ approaches infinity for any actuating force. If:

\begin{equation}
\boxed{l_1 \leq \mu \cdot l_2}
(\#eq:self-locking-14)
\end{equation}

then $F \leq 0$: the friction force alone maintains full shoe-drum engagement without any actuating force. This is called **self-locking** or **self-actuation**.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Self-locking condition. When this inequality holds, the brake engages and holds under friction alone — no actuating force is required (or can release the shoe without mechanical intervention).](images/drTrain-image20.png){fig-align='center' width=28%}
:::
:::


**Self-locking in practice:**

- **Automotive brakes:** Self-locking is deliberately **avoided**. Geometry is designed so that $\mu_{critical} = l_1 / l_2 \gg \mu_{actual}$, giving a large safety margin. A self-locking automotive brake cannot be released by the driver.
- **Industrial fail-safe brakes:** Self-locking is deliberately **designed in** for crane hoists, mine winches, and elevator safety brakes. If hydraulic power fails, the brake engages automatically and holds the load.

#### Worked Examples {#sec:shoe-brake-examples-14}

##### Example 1: Leading Shoe — Normal Force and Braking Torque

**Given:**
- $l = 200$ mm, $l_1 = 120$ mm, $l_2 = 30$ mm
- $\mu = 0.35$, drum radius $r = 150$ mm
- Applied actuating force: $F = 200$ N *(leading shoe)*

**Find:** Normal force $F_N$ and braking torque $T$.

**Solution:**

Rearrange Equation \@ref(eq:leading-shoe-14) for $F_N$:

$$F_N = \frac{F \cdot l}{l_1 - \mu \cdot l_2} = \frac{200 \times 0.200}{0.120 - 0.35 \times 0.030} = \frac{40.0}{0.120 - 0.0105} = \frac{40.0}{0.1095}$$

$$\boxed{F_N = 365.3 \text{ N}}$$

Braking torque from Equation \@ref(eq:shoe-torque-14):

$$T = \mu \cdot F_N \cdot r = 0.35 \times 365.3 \times 0.150 = \boxed{19.2 \text{ N·m}}$$

---

##### Example 2: Trailing Shoe — Same Geometry

**Given:** Same geometry and applied force as Example 1, but now a **trailing shoe**.

**Solution:**

$$F_N = \frac{F \cdot l}{l_1 + \mu \cdot l_2} = \frac{40.0}{0.120 + 0.0105} = \frac{40.0}{0.1305} = \boxed{306.5 \text{ N}}$$

$$T = 0.35 \times 306.5 \times 0.150 = \boxed{16.1 \text{ N·m}}$$

**Comparison:** With the same actuating force, the leading shoe produces **19% more normal force** and **19% more braking torque**. This explains why leading shoes wear faster in a leading-trailing arrangement.

---

##### Example 3: Self-Locking Check

**Given:** Leading shoe with $l_1 = 120$ mm, $l_2 = 30$ mm, $\mu = 0.35$.

Check: $l_1 \leq \mu \cdot l_2$?

$$0.120 \leq 0.35 \times 0.030 = 0.0105? \quad \Rightarrow \quad 0.120 > 0.0105$$

**Self-locking does NOT occur.** The critical $\mu$ for self-locking:

$$\mu_{critical} = \frac{l_1}{l_2} = \frac{120}{30} = 4.0 \quad \text{(physically impossible for any brake lining)}$$

This geometry has a very large safety margin — intentionally so for an automotive application.

---

##### Example 4: Complete Drum Brake — Leading and Trailing Combined

**Given:** One leading and one trailing shoe, same wheel cylinder force per shoe:
- $F = 350$ N per shoe, $l = 180$ mm, $l_1 = 110$ mm, $l_2 = 25$ mm
- $\mu = 0.38$, $r = 140$ mm

**Find:** Total braking torque.

*Leading shoe:*
$$F_{N,L} = \frac{350 \times 0.180}{0.110 - 0.38 \times 0.025} = \frac{63.0}{0.1005} = 626.9 \text{ N}, \quad T_L = 0.38 \times 626.9 \times 0.140 = 33.3 \text{ N·m}$$

*Trailing shoe:*
$$F_{N,T} = \frac{350 \times 0.180}{0.110 + 0.38 \times 0.025} = \frac{63.0}{0.1195} = 527.2 \text{ N}, \quad T_T = 0.38 \times 527.2 \times 0.140 = 28.0 \text{ N·m}$$

$$\boxed{T_{total} = 33.3 + 28.0 = 61.3 \text{ N·m}}$$


