### Anti-Lock Braking System (ABS) {#sec:abs-14}

::: {.callout-think}
**Think About It:** Why does locking up a wheel reduce your ability to steer *and* increase stopping distance? What is actually happening at the tyre contact patch when the wheel locks?
:::

The **Anti-lock Braking System (ABS)** is one of the most significant active safety innovations in automotive history. It prevents wheel lockup during hard braking, maintaining steering control and typically reducing stopping distances on most surfaces.

#### Why Wheel Lockup Is Dangerous {#sec:wheel-lockup-14}

When a wheel locks (stops rotating while the vehicle is still moving), the tyre transitions from **rolling friction** to **sliding friction**:

- **Rolling tyre:** The contact patch generates both longitudinal braking force and lateral (steering) force
- **Locked tyre:** The contact patch generates only longitudinal braking force in the direction of sliding; **lateral force drops to near zero**

A locked front wheel means the vehicle will not respond to steering inputs — it understeers straight regardless of steering wheel angle. A locked rear wheel can cause the vehicle to spin.

Additionally, peak braking force occurs at a **slip ratio** of approximately 10–20%, not at 100% slip (locked). A rolling tyre with controlled slip generates *more* longitudinal braking force than a fully locked tyre, especially on wet, icy, or loose surfaces.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Tyre friction coefficient versus wheel slip ratio. Both longitudinal (braking) and lateral (steering) friction coefficients are shown. Peak braking force occurs at 10–20% slip. At 100% slip (locked wheel), longitudinal force is reduced and lateral force drops to near zero — the vehicle cannot be steered.](02-11-Brakes_files/figure-epub/abs-slip-curve-14-1.png){fig-align='center'}
:::
:::


#### How ABS Works {#sec:abs-operation-14}

ABS uses **wheel speed sensors** at each wheel to monitor rotational speed. During braking:

1. The ECU compares individual wheel speeds to the estimated vehicle speed
2. If a wheel decelerates much faster than the others (indicating impending lockup), the ABS ECU commands the **ABS hydraulic modulator** to reduce brake pressure at that wheel
3. The wheel accelerates slightly; when it recovers speed, pressure is re-applied
4. This **pressure modulate cycle** repeats 10–15 times per second per wheel

The driver hears and feels a characteristic pulsing in the brake pedal — this is normal ABS operation, not a fault. The correct technique with ABS is to apply **firm, continuous** pedal pressure and let the system manage individual wheel slip.

**ABS hardware:**

| Component | Function |
|:----------|:---------|
| **Wheel Speed Sensors** | Toothed reluctor ring + magnetic sensor at each wheel; generates pulses proportional to wheel speed |
| **ABS ECU** | Computes wheel slip; generates commands to the modulator |
| **Hydraulic Modulator** | Solenoid valves and a return pump; can independently hold, reduce, or restore pressure at each wheel circuit |

::: {.callout-industry}
**Industry Application:** ABS has been mandatory on all new passenger vehicles sold in the European Union since 2004, and in Canada/USA since 2013. Assembly plant end-of-line (EOL) testing includes an ABS functional test where each solenoid valve is pulsed and confirmed via pressure sensors. If the modulator block fails the functional test, the vehicle is quarantined for investigation — the ABS module cannot be shipped with a fault.
:::

