### Braking Performance Calculations {#sec:braking-performance-14}

Understanding braking performance quantitatively is essential for vehicle dynamics, brake system sizing, and safety analysis.

#### Stopping Distance {#sec:stopping-distance-14}

The **minimum stopping distance** of a vehicle braking from initial speed $v_0$ to rest, assuming constant deceleration $a$:

\begin{equation}
d = \frac{v_0^2}{2a}
(\#eq:stopping-dist-14)
\end{equation}

The maximum deceleration is limited by tyre-road friction:

\begin{equation}
a_{max} = \mu_p \cdot g
(\#eq:max-decel-14)
\end{equation}

where $\mu_p$ is the peak tyre-road friction coefficient and $g = 9.81$ m/s².

Combining:

\begin{equation}
d_{min} = \frac{v_0^2}{2 \mu_p g}
(\#eq:min-stop-14)
\end{equation}

**Worked Example — Stopping Distance:**

A car travelling at 100 km/h ($v_0 = 27.8$ m/s) brakes to a stop on dry asphalt ($\mu_p = 0.85$):

$$d_{min} = \frac{(27.8)^2}{2 \times 0.85 \times 9.81} = \frac{772.8}{16.68} = \boxed{46.3 \text{ m}}$$

On wet asphalt ($\mu_p = 0.55$):

$$d_{min} = \frac{772.8}{2 \times 0.55 \times 9.81} = \frac{772.8}{10.79} = \boxed{71.6 \text{ m}}$$

The **total stopping distance** also includes **reaction distance** — the distance travelled during driver reaction time before braking begins:

\begin{equation}
d_{total} = v_0 \cdot t_r + \frac{v_0^2}{2\mu_p g}
(\#eq:total-stop-14)
\end{equation}

where $t_r$ is the driver reaction time (typically 1.0–1.5 s for alert drivers; up to 2.5 s for distracted drivers).

At 100 km/h with $t_r = 1.2$ s and $\mu_p = 0.85$:

$$d_{total} = 27.8 \times 1.2 + 46.3 = 33.4 + 46.3 = \boxed{79.7 \text{ m}}$$

#### Braking Force and Required Brake Torque {#sec:brake-force-14}

The total **braking force** required to decelerate the vehicle:

\begin{equation}
F_{brake,total} = m \cdot a
(\#eq:total-brake-force-14)
\end{equation}

This force is distributed between the front and rear axles. For a vehicle with weight distribution $\phi_f$ (fraction of weight on front axle):

\begin{equation}
F_{front} = F_{brake,total} \cdot \phi_f, \qquad F_{rear} = F_{brake,total} \cdot (1 - \phi_f)
(\#eq:brake-distribution-14)
\end{equation}

(In practice, $\phi_f$ increases with deceleration due to weight transfer — see Section \@ref(sec:ebd-14).)

The required **brake torque per front wheel** (with $r_w$ = rolling radius):

\begin{equation}
T_{wheel} = \frac{F_{front}}{2} \cdot r_w
(\#eq:wheel-torque-14)
\end{equation}

**Worked Example — Brake Torque Required:**

A 1,600 kg car decelerates at $0.8g = 7.85$ m/s² from 100 km/h. Static weight distribution is 60% front. Rolling radius $r_w = 0.32$ m.

Total braking force:
$$F_{brake} = 1{,}600 \times 7.85 = 12{,}560 \text{ N}$$

Front axle braking force (ignoring weight transfer for simplicity):
$$F_{front} = 12{,}560 \times 0.60 = 7{,}536 \text{ N}$$

Required brake torque per front wheel:
$$T_{wheel} = \frac{7{,}536}{2} \times 0.32 = 3{,}768 \times 0.32 = \boxed{1{,}206 \text{ N·m}}$$


