**Chapter Summary — Can you answer these questions?**

1. A tandem master cylinder has two separate hydraulic circuits. What is the safety rationale for having two circuits rather than one?

2. A leading shoe generates more braking force than a trailing shoe with the same actuating force. What is the mechanical cause of this difference, and why does it result in uneven shoe wear?

3. The ABS system pulses brake pressure 10–15 times per second. Why does this produce *more* braking force than simply locking the wheel and allowing it to slide?

4. EBD dynamically adjusts front-rear brake force distribution. Why must the rear brakes receive less pressure than the front brakes during a hard stop, even if both axles have identical brake hardware?

5. A fully charged EV battery limits regenerative braking capability. Why, and what happens to the braking performance of the vehicle as a result?

6. A vehicle brakes from 120 km/h on wet asphalt ($\mu = 0.55$). Calculate the minimum braking distance. If driver reaction time is 1.5 s, what is the total stopping distance?
